// PIN — `expr: ?Expression` is refused AT THE DECLARATION.
//
// `expr` is not a parameter name, it is a syntactic ROLE: the binding site for
// the argument the author writes without a label. Optionality means "omit the
// label and get null", and for `expr` omitting the label is the normal case —
// so `?` would have to mean something different there than it means on every
// other field of the same shape.
//
// It is also unreachable. Under the first-positional rule there is no syntax
// for "skip the positional but keep the later ones" (`f(, n: 1)` is not a
// thing), so the only call an optional `expr` could ever enable is the bare
// `f()` — and a tor whose expression may simply be absent wants a named
// parameter, not the slot.
//
// Measured 2026-08-02: this was accepted and the `?` was silently DROPPED — the
// emitted Input carried `expr: []const u8`, not `?[]const u8`. Ruled a
// declaration-time refusal (Lars) so it fires on the library author rather than
// on their users. A named `<other>: ?Expression` stays legal — see 400_184.
~import std/io

~[expand]pub tor shout { expr: ?Expression }

~proc shout|template|zig {
{{ expr }}
}

~shout("hi")
